{"content":[{"c":["FIRSTBORN"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Term used in the Bible to describe a family’s oldest son or daughter (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.22.21","r":"Gen.22.21","rawR":"Gen.22.21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.22.21","value":"Gen.22.21"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 22:21"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.29.26","r":"Gen.29.26","rawR":"Gen.29.26","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.29.26","value":"Gen.29.26"}],"t":"ref","v":"29:26"},"). Israel was called God’s firstborn because of that nation’s miraculous beginning and special deliverance out of Egypt (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.17.5,15-16","r":"Gen.17.5,15-16","rawR":"Gen.17.5,15-16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.17.5,15-16","value":"Gen.17.5,15-16"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 17:5, 15-16"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.4.22","r":"Exod.4.22","rawR":"Exod.4.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.4.22","value":"Exod.4.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ex 4:22"},"). As God’s firstborn, Israel had unique privileges over all other nations. Gentiles were “blessed” only in relation to their kindness to Israel (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.12.3","r":"Gen.12.3","rawR":"Gen.12.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.12.3","value":"Gen.12.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 12:3"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.19.6","r":"Exod.19.6","rawR":"Exod.19.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.19.6","value":"Exod.19.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ex 19:6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.4.5-8","r":"Deut.4.5-8","rawR":"Deut.4.5-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.4.5-8","value":"Deut.4.5-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Dt 4:5-8"},"). The prophet Isaiah foresaw a day when Israel would have a double portion of inheritance (",{"href":"?bref=Isa.61.7","r":"Isa.61.7","rawR":"Isa.61.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.61.7","value":"Isa.61.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Is 61:7"},"). Thus, being firstborn implies priority or preeminence, as well as an inheritance."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["The expression “first-born of the poor,” (",{"href":"?bref=Isa.14.30","r":"Isa.14.30","rawR":"Isa.14.30","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.14.30","value":"Isa.14.30"}],"t":"ref","v":"Is 14:30"},", ",{"c":["rsv"],"sourceClass":"sc","t":"span"},") means one who is supremely poor, the poorest of the poor. Another figurative expression, “first-born of death” (",{"href":"?bref=Job.18.13","r":"Job.18.13","rawR":"Job.18.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.18.13","value":"Job.18.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jb 18:13"},", ",{"c":["rsv"],"sourceClass":"sc","t":"span"},"), implied that Job’s disease was fatal."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["Because God delivered Israel’s firstborn sons from death in Egypt, he expected each firstborn to be sanctified to him (",{"href":"?bref=Exod.11.4-7","r":"Exod.11.4-7","rawR":"Exod.11.4-7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.11.4-7","value":"Exod.11.4-7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ex 11:4-7"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.13.12","r":"Exod.13.12","rawR":"Exod.13.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.13.12","value":"Exod.13.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"13:12"},"). The first male child was a representative of the entire offspring (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.49.3","r":"Gen.49.3","rawR":"Gen.49.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.49.3","value":"Gen.49.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 49:3"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.22.29","r":"Exod.22.29","rawR":"Exod.22.29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.22.29","value":"Exod.22.29"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ex 22:29"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Num.3.13","r":"Num.3.13","rawR":"Num.3.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.3.13","value":"Num.3.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Nm 3:13"},"). The firstborn of all animals used in sacrifice was to be sanctified to the Lord (",{"href":"?bref=Exod.13.2,15","r":"Exod.13.2,15","rawR":"Exod.13.2,15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.13.2,15","value":"Exod.13.2,15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ex 13:2, 15"},")."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["Firstborn and Redemption"],"sourceClass":"h2","t":"p"},{"c":["The firstborn of every tribe except Levi’s was to be redeemed by a sum not to exceed five shekels (",{"href":"?bref=Num.18.15-16","r":"Num.18.15-16","rawR":"Num.18.15-16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.18.15-16","value":"Num.18.15-16"}],"t":"ref","v":"Nm 18:15-16"},"). Redemption implied a previous bondage and was to remind Israel of their redemption from bondage in Egypt (",{"href":"?bref=Exod.13.2-8","r":"Exod.13.2-8","rawR":"Exod.13.2-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.13.2-8","value":"Exod.13.2-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ex 13:2-8"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["The firstborn of ritually clean animals was devoted to the Lord. It was brought to the tabernacle (or later, the temple) within a year from the eighth day after birth. This animal was then sacrificed and its blood sprinkled on the altar. The meat of the sacrificed animal was for the priests (",{"href":"?bref=Exod.13.13","r":"Exod.13.13","rawR":"Exod.13.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.13.13","value":"Exod.13.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ex 13:13"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.22.30","r":"Exod.22.30","rawR":"Exod.22.30","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.22.30","value":"Exod.22.30"}],"t":"ref","v":"22:30"},"; cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Num.18.17","r":"Num.18.17","rawR":"Num.18.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.18.17","value":"Num.18.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"Nm 18:17"},"). The firstborn of unclean animals could be redeemed with an addition of one-fifth of the value as determined by the priest. If not redeemed, these animals were sold, exchanged, or destroyed by the priests (",{"href":"?bref=Lev.27.27","r":"Lev.27.27","rawR":"Lev.27.27","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.27.27","value":"Lev.27.27"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lv 27:27"},"). The colt of an ass was to be redeemed with a lamb (",{"href":"?bref=Exod.13.13","r":"Exod.13.13","rawR":"Exod.13.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.13.13","value":"Exod.13.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ex 13:13"},"). If not redeemed, it was to be killed. Meat from unclean animals was not eaten."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["Firstborn and Birthright"],"sourceClass":"h2","t":"p"},{"c":["The firstborn acted as priest of the family in the father’s absence or death. Esau and Reuben are both examples (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.27.19,32","r":"Gen.27.19,32","rawR":"Gen.27.19,32","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.27.19,32","value":"Gen.27.19,32"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 27:19, 32"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.5.1-2","r":"1Chr.5.1-2","rawR":"1Chr.5.1-2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.5.1-2","value":"1Chr.5.1-2"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Chr 5:1-2"},"). This position of the firstborn ceased when the priesthood was committed to Levi’s tribe (",{"href":"?bref=Num.3.12-13","r":"Num.3.12-13","rawR":"Num.3.12-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.3.12-13","value":"Num.3.12-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Nm 3:12-13"},"). All the firstborn of succeeding generations had to be redeemed. The redemption money became part of the Levites’ yearly income (",{"href":"?bref=Num.8.17","r":"Num.8.17","rawR":"Num.8.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.8.17","value":"Num.8.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"8:17"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Num.18.16","r":"Num.18.16","rawR":"Num.18.16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.18.16","value":"Num.18.16"}],"t":"ref","v":"18:16"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["A double portion of the family inheritance was the right of the firstborn. This protected the firstborn when there was a polygamous marriage. The son of a favorite wife could not take the place of the first son born of the household (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.21.17","r":"Deut.21.17","rawR":"Deut.21.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.21.17","value":"Deut.21.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"Dt 21:17"},")."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["The title “firstborn” is applied to Christ (",{"href":"?bref=Luke.2.7","r":"Luke.2.7","rawR":"Luke.2.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Luke.2.7","value":"Luke.2.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lk 2:7"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Rom.8.29","r":"Rom.8.29","rawR":"Rom.8.29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Rom.8.29","value":"Rom.8.29"}],"t":"ref","v":"Rom 8:29"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Col.1.15,18","r":"Col.1.15,18","rawR":"Col.1.15,18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Col.1.15,18","value":"Col.1.15,18"}],"t":"ref","v":"Col 1:15, 18"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Heb.1.6","r":"Heb.1.6","rawR":"Heb.1.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Heb.1.6","value":"Heb.1.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Heb 1:6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Rev.1.5","r":"Rev.1.5","rawR":"Rev.1.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Rev.1.5","value":"Rev.1.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"Rv 1:5"},"). It stresses Christ’s preeminence over all because he was the first to rise from the dead. As firstborn, Christ is heir of all things (",{"href":"?bref=Heb.1.2","r":"Heb.1.2","rawR":"Heb.1.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Heb.1.2","value":"Heb.1.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"Heb 1:2"},") and the head of the church (",{"href":"?bref=Eph.1.20-23","r":"Eph.1.20-23","rawR":"Eph.1.20-23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eph.1.20-23","value":"Eph.1.20-23"}],"t":"ref","v":"Eph 1:20-23"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Col.1.18,24","r":"Col.1.18,24","rawR":"Col.1.18,24","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Col.1.18,24","value":"Col.1.18,24"}],"t":"ref","v":"Col 1:18, 24"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Heb.2.10-12","r":"Heb.2.10-12","rawR":"Heb.2.10-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Heb.2.10-12","value":"Heb.2.10-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Heb 2:10-12"},")."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":[{"c":["See also"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," ",{"href":"?item=Birthright_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"Birthright_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"Birthright_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Birthright"},"; ",{"href":"?item=Heir_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"Heir_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"Heir_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Heir"},"; ",{"href":"?item=Inheritance_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"Inheritance_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"Inheritance_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Inheritance"},"; ",{"href":"?item=Primogeniture_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"Primogeniture_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"Primogeniture_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Primogeniture"},"."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Firstborn","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Gn 22:21","canonicalization"